// Bare metal's address-of is emit-identity-builtin: it returns the VALUE,
// and since records, lists and texts are pointers there, the value IS the
// address.
//
// HEAP-RELATIVE, not a host pointer: the answers are compared against
// __heap-save values, which are offsets from cx_heap_base. A raw @intFromPtr
// would order correctly among itself and be nonsense against those.
fn cx_address_of(v: anytype) i64 {
    switch (@typeInfo(@TypeOf(v))) {
        .int, .comptime_int => return @intCast(v),
        .bool => return @intFromBool(v),
        .pointer => |cx_pi| {
            // AN EMPTY SLICE HAS NO POINTER. Zig leaves .ptr undefined when
            // len is 0 -- safe builds fill it with 0xaa -- so asking a
            // zero-length Text where it lives reads poison, and @intCast traps
            // on the distance from the region to a pointer that was never one.
            // 0 is the right answer rather than a lie: mcopy-text reads it as
            // "not in the copied region, share as-is", and an empty text is
            // exactly what needs no copy.
            //
            // 0 IS THE "NO ADDRESS" SENTINEL, so a real address must never be
            // 0. A source literal lives in .rodata, below the heap, and the
            // displacement is SIGNED so it answers a distinct negative:
            // `a == 0` stays false while `a < mc.mc-floor` and `address-of t
            // < b` are true, which is what a literal wants -- it never moves,
            // so sharing it is durable and copying it would be waste. Offset 0
            // is not a live object and so cannot collide with the empty-slice
            // sentinel: cx_hp starts at 6291456, and everything under it
            // belongs to the deck.
            if (cx_pi.size == .slice and v.len == 0) return 0;
            const cx_p = if (cx_pi.size == .slice) @intFromPtr(v.ptr) else @intFromPtr(v);
